<Docs
  path="/docs/providers"
  heading="Providers"
  lead="KalCode works with the AI coding tools you already use, on your own accounts. Connecting them is free on every plan."
>
  <h2>Supported providers</h2>
  <p>Every coding agent in KalCode is the provider's real CLI, in its own terminal:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Claude Code</strong> — the local CLI, signed in with your own Claude account.</li>
    <li><strong>Codex</strong> — the local CLI, signed in with your own ChatGPT account, including Business, Enterprise and Edu accounts.</li>
    <li><strong>Cursor</strong> — Cursor Agent in an interactive terminal, signed in with your Cursor account, with the models that account offers.</li>
    <li><strong>Gemini CLI</strong> — the local CLI, signed in with Google's own sign-in, running in your real workspace.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Every provider is included on every plan. More providers can be added later through a documented
    adapter contract, and each integration is verified against the provider's current official
    documentation before it ships.
  </p>

  <h2>Native, as in your own terminal</h2>
  <p>
    Agents keep their native tools. They search and fetch the web (in Plan mode too), use your own
    MCP servers, and run their shell, file and repo-search tools, plugins and slash commands. Each
    provider reads its own setup: settings, <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, <code>AGENTS.md</code> and
    <code>GEMINI.md</code>, agents, skills, plugins and commands. Codex uses your own
    <code>config.toml</code>, project trust and rules files, and can run up to ten sub-agents.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Providers page lists each provider's tools as native, needs setup, or not offered, with the
    reason, and a coding pane shows which tool is running.
  </p>

  <h2>How connections work</h2>
  <p>
    KalCode uses only legitimate, documented integration methods: each provider's own local CLI,
    signed in through that provider's official sign-in flow. It never scrapes browser credentials,
    copies hidden session credentials, or reverse-engineers private authentication.
  </p>

  <h2>Signing in</h2>
  <p>
    Sign in to each provider inside KalCode: open Providers, then Accounts, add an account and choose
    Sign in. KalCode runs that provider's own official sign-in for that account only. Claude Code and
    Codex open their browser sign-in, Cursor runs its own sign-in, and Gemini CLI opens Google sign-in
    in your browser.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each account has its own managed provider profile. Signing a CLI in from a separate terminal signs
    in a different profile that KalCode doesn't use, so there is nothing to run by hand. The provider
    keeps the credentials in that account's profile and KalCode never sees them. Gemini CLI keeps its
    sign-in in its own encrypted credential store, never in plaintext. Gemini derives that store's key
    from your computer and user names, so it keeps the sign-in out of plaintext but not away from
    other programs running as your user.
  </p>
  <p>
    Your usage of each provider is billed by that provider under your account, exactly as it is when
    you use it directly. KalCode does not charge for, resell or meter provider usage, holds no
    company provider credentials for your use, and has no KalCode-paid fallback. KalVoice does not
    use your provider accounts to interpret commands: interpretation runs on your computer, and
    there is no provider fallback.
  </p>

  <h2>Supported CLI versions</h2>
  <p>
    Managed accounts run the provider CLI release lines KalCode has certified: Claude Code 2.1.282
    or a later 2.1 release, Codex CLI 0.160 (on macOS also the 0.155 to 0.159 lines, from 0.155.1),
    and Gemini CLI 0.61. Cursor runs the Cursor Agent CLI you install. Pre-release builds aren't
    supported. With any other version, sign-in and agents stop with a message that names the
    supported versions.
  </p>

  <h2>Several accounts per provider</h2>
  <p>
    You can add more than one account for each provider, such as a personal and a work account.
    Providers, then Accounts, shows each account and the workspaces where it is the default.
    <strong>Set default</strong> chooses the account new agents use where a workspace has no default
    of its own, and <strong>Connect another account</strong> adds an account and starts its sign-in.
  </p>
  <p>
    In the <strong>New agent</strong> launcher you choose the provider, account, model and effort for
    each agent, and launch several at once. Every agent shows its exact provider, account, model and
    effort.
  </p>

  <h2>Switching an agent's account</h2>
  <p>
    Each agent stays on the account it started with and never changes account on its own. The
    account picker in a coding terminal's header opens Account &amp; usage for that agent; choosing a
    different account starts a fresh session on it and keeps the original. When an account is
    limited, KalCode suggests another account you connected, and switches only when you choose to.
  </p>

  <h2>Detection</h2>
  <p>
    To find installed providers, KalCode looks for their executables on your <code>PATH</code> and in
    documented install locations, then runs only each CLI's version command, with a timeout. It
    reports whether each provider is installed, its version and what it can do. Detection doesn't
    check sign-in: whether each account is signed in shows on its card in Providers, then Accounts.
    Detection never changes your machine; installing a provider only happens when you ask for it.
  </p>

  <h2>Your environment</h2>
  <p>
    Each agent starts with your real environment, the way it would in your own terminal: your SSH
    agent, <code>GH_TOKEN</code>, toolchain homes, cloud credentials and the variables your MCP servers
    use. KalCode leaves out only its own internal variables and, for a managed account, the few
    variables that would sign that provider in as a different account. Each provider session runs in
    its own supervised process: if one fails, the others, and your terminals, workspaces and
    Dashboard, keep running.
  </p>

  <h2>One model for every provider</h2>
  <p>
    Provider details are translated at the adapter boundary into KalCode's own concepts: one agent
    state (starting, working, waiting, needs you, ready, done or failed), normalized events and
    capabilities. That is what lets the Dashboard, Needs You and KalVoice treat every provider the
    same way.
  </p>
</Docs>
